Z||000000||CUTTER||04-24-2000||03:08 AM||||I wrecked the front of my car over the winter and recently got a gold front on my blue87GT. I figured If I sand it lightly and just spray it with auto paint it would look better then it did. So I went to the auto store bought some auto paint and matched the best I could, bought sandpaper and some clearcoat under $15 went home and started to tape everything up. sanded it down and put about 6 light coats of paint on. I thought WOW it looks perfesional. the next day I clearcoated it and it looks great. Its a little darker but hey, for $15 Its a deal. I also did the same to my spoiler because it was faded as heck. And did the same to my ground effects, Looks great! no runs or anything, anyone know how reliable this method is and how long the paint will last? I am thiking of doing the same around the sunroof from sun fading. Good idea? if not what do I have to buy, or do so I can do my paintjob my self and it wont cost alot and will last at least the rest of the year so I can save for a profesional paint job.<BR>Thanks alot, Shane||||reg||1
Z||000001||sdm1964||04-24-2000||08:22 AM||||I see no reason why you could not do the whole car that way. It is the same type of paint. With the proper prep their should be no difference. I redid the dash of a 61 amc rambler, sanded, primered and paint then clear coat. It looked good for years.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||04-24-2000||04:28 PM||||sdm,<P>I can't believe you said, "AMC Rambler" and "looked good" in the same sentence!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick86GT||04-24-2000||06:35 PM||||i done enuff auto painting over the years to be dangerous. that said, the paint will probably hold but i do know that all paint on a Fiero lasts best if it contains a special flex agent that permits just what the name implies.  otherwise, bumping the paint/body panels can cause cracks and other unsightly things<P>now i confess. fixing up an 87 coupe for my kid, i bought spray cans of match color & clear coat at pep-boys.  the result was acceptable (no runs, no drips, no errors) but the color varied from original.  for my own 86GT paint job, i hired it done!||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||04-24-2000||06:58 PM||||It's the principal of the thing that kills me... The whole idea of painting my fiero with spray cans sounds bad.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Boodoggin||04-24-2000||11:00 PM||||I bought 4 dollar chrome spray from Wal Mart and cleaned and sanded my exhaust tips.  I covered the area around them and sprayed them with a heavy coating.  It looks unbelievably awesome!!  It made my faded exhaust tips look like brand new after market.  Best $4 I ever spent. ||||reg||14
Z||000006||Capt Fiero||04-24-2000||11:06 PM||||How does this spray clearcoat work.  I would like to try some.  Any recomendations on what to or not to buy.  I bought some spare parts for my car after my accident. got a 20$ hood, and some extra stuff real cheap, but the paint was in poor shape, I shot it all with trim clad gloss black paint 4 coats and it looks good, but I would like to get it semi shinny.  Will that spray on clearcoat do that.||||reg||1
Z||000007||CUTTER||04-25-2000||01:47 AM||||Thats what I did and it looks good I rate it a 8 outa 10. Go to your local auto parts store and say something to them, they will give you a good answer!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fformula88||04-25-2000||04:09 PM||||You can do a very good job with the spray cans...  I have done a few jobs this way with varying degrees of success.  My only worry with painting a whole car this way is running into variations in color from can to can.  It would take a lot of them to do a whole car, and if they came from different batches at the factory, they may vary.  <P>To get the finish shinier, you need to do some buffing at a minimum.  If the surface feels slightly rough, you may need to do some wet sanding using 1000 + grit paper.  Nothing real course.  Then get yourself a light buffing compound... I usually use some that 3M makes, and a buffing disc.  You can use a drill if you don't have a buffer, just put on the compound and buff lightly on it.  It will come out VERY smooth and shiney.<P>I did a new spoiler for my 88 Formula this way, and the color matched almost perfectly.||||reg||12
Z||000009||lowCG||04-25-2000||05:40 PM||||I painted my Austin Mini with Krylon,and people were asking "who did the paint job";I could hardly keep a straight face.<BR>It looked great,but required a waxing every week to keep it looking real nice.||||reg||1
